From 349d081b62ce2e3f5184ec3e0d5d100bde78dc09 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Damien Dusha <Damien Dusha>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 23:37:17 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] lwIP NSD: untag the pbuf before releasing it

ptp_nsd_transmit_msg() sets p->tag and p->tx_cb and never clears them:

    p->tag   = (void *)uid;
    p->tx_cb = ptp_transmit_cb;
    ...
    pbuf_free(p);

Both live in LWIP_PBUF_CUSTOM_DATA, which lwIP does not initialise on
allocation -- it manages its own fields and nothing else -- so the values
survive into whatever allocation next lands on that memory. A port that gates
hardware transmit timestamping on `p->tx_cb != NULL`, which is the only signal
this driver offers, then requests a hardware timestamp for a frame that never
asked for one and calls ptp_transmit_timestamp_cb() with a stale uid.

Nothing consumes those, so they queue on sTxCbFIFO until it is full and are
then dropped. Since e873105 that is a counted drop; before it, it was a
blocking enqueue from an ISR on the one firmware where blocking meant a
circular wait on LOCK_TCPIP_CORE.

Measured on a NUCLEO-H563ZI running the FreeRTOS + lwIP demo, 120 s of
saturated link (~8.5 M packets, ~75 000 pps, ~78 Mbit/s):

    before:  ptp.drop_txts = 284392
    after:   ptp.drop_txts = 0

with the transmit-timestamp check still passing either way, so the genuine t3
timestamps are unaffected -- only the spurious ones are gone.

The count tracks TRANSMIT volume, not receive pressure, which is what
identifies it. A flood of multicast the node does not reply to produces none;
adding a unicast stream that provokes ICMP port-unreachable replies produced
42531 in 30 s. The ThreadX + NetX Duo firmware, which supplies its own driver
and passes the uid through a side channel rather than on the buffer, produced
0 throughout.

The send is synchronous -- udp_sendto() and ethernet_output() reach
netif->linkoutput before returning -- so both values have already been taken
by the time they are cleared.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../port/example_netstack_drivers/nsd_lwip.c       | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/flexptp/port/example_netstack_drivers/nsd_lwip.c b/src/flexptp/port/example_netstack_drivers/nsd_lwip.c
index 2811990..f6d25cf 100644
--- a/src/flexptp/port/example_netstack_drivers/nsd_lwip.c
+++ b/src/flexptp/port/example_netstack_drivers/nsd_lwip.c
@@ -188,6 +188,20 @@ void ptp_nsd_transmit_msg(RawPtpMessage *pMsg, uint32_t uid) {
     // unlock LWIP core
     UNLOCK_TCPIP_CORE();
 
+    /* Untag the pbuf before releasing it.
+     *
+     * tx_cb and tag live in LWIP_PBUF_CUSTOM_DATA, which lwIP does not initialise on
+     * allocation -- it manages its own fields and nothing else. So whatever is left here
+     * survives into the next allocation that lands on this memory, and a port that gates
+     * hardware transmit timestamping on `p->tx_cb != NULL`, which is the only signal this
+     * driver offers, then requests a timestamp for a frame that never asked for one and
+     * reports it against a stale uid.
+     *
+     * The send above is synchronous -- udp_sendto() and ethernet_output() reach
+     * netif->linkoutput before returning -- so the driver has already taken both values. */
+    p->tx_cb = NULL;
+    p->tag = NULL;
+
     pbuf_free(p); // release buffer
 }
 
